---
import { Download } from '@lucide/astro'
import { release } from '@/data/release'

const command = 'Get-FileHash .\\Notch-Setup.exe -Algorithm SHA256'
---

<section class="section install" id="install">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Install</span>
      <h2 class="h2">Running in about a minute.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        One file, no account. The build is unsigned, so Windows will ask once — here is exactly what
        it says and why.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <span class="num">01</span>
        <h3>Download</h3>
        <p>
          Grab the installer for a Start-menu shortcut, or the portable build if you would rather
          not install anything.
        </p>
        <a class="link" href="/download">Choose a build <Download size={13} /></a>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <span class="num">02</span>
        <h3>Run it</h3>
        <p>
          Windows shows “unknown publisher” because the build is not code-signed. Choose
          <strong>More info → Run anyway</strong>, or verify the hash first.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <span class="num">03</span>
        <h3>Hover the top edge</h3>
        <p>
          The notch starts collapsed. Hover the top centre of your screen, or click the tab. Press
          Esc to fold it back.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="code-block">
      <button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy={command}>Copy</button>
      <span class="comment"># compare with SHA256SUMS.txt from the release page</span><br />
      <span class="prompt">PS&gt;</span> {command}
    </div>

    <div class="accordion">
      <details>
        <summary>Why does Windows warn me?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          The app is not code-signed. A certificate costs money every year and this is a free
          project, so Windows shows the standard “unknown publisher” prompt. Every release is built
          by a public GitHub Actions workflow and publishes a SHA-256 checksum you can verify before
          running anything.
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is Smart App Control?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          A Windows 11 security setting that blocks unsigned apps outright. If it is on, the
          installer may be stopped before it runs. The portable build often works, but if Windows
          still blocks it, turning Smart App Control off is a system-wide security decision that
          only you should make. The app is the same either way — it is the signing that is missing.
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Installer or portable?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          Both contain the same app. The installer ({release.setup.sizeMb} MB) adds a Start-menu
          shortcut and an uninstaller. The portable build ({release.portable.sizeMb} MB) runs from
          wherever you put it and leaves nothing behind except your settings in
          <code>%APPDATA%\notch</code>.
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does it auto-update?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          Not yet. The app shows its version, and the site and GitHub releases always carry the
          latest build, so updating is a download away.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
